1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,910
You have the right to remain silent.

2
00:00:02,990 --> 00:00:06,650
You've heard it on every
cop show for fifty years.

3
00:00:06,730 --> 00:00:08,770
Here's what you haven't heard.

4
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:12,870
The man those words are named
after was convicted anyway.

5
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:17,790
Twice. And when he was killed
in a bar fight in 1976,

6
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:21,290
the man accused of killing
him was never tried.

7
00:00:21,370 --> 00:00:23,950
The reason wasn't Miranda.

8
00:00:24,242 --> 00:00:26,812
I'm Eric Hoffman, and I'm a lawyer.

9
00:00:26,892 --> 00:00:29,552
I've got a case that almost
every American can recite,

10
00:00:29,632 --> 00:00:32,312
but almost nobody really knows.

11
00:00:32,392 --> 00:00:33,500
Let's open the file.

12
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:42,980
March 1963. Phoenix.

13
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:46,700
An eighteen-year-old woman finishes
her shift at a movie theater,

14
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:50,680
takes the bus home, and is
abducted and raped on the way.

15
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:54,117
We won't show her,
and we won't use her name.

16
00:00:55,275 --> 00:01:01,165
About a week later, a relative spots a car
that matches her description and gets part

17
00:01:01,245 --> 00:01:07,742
of the plate. It leads police to a man in
his early twenties named Ernesto Miranda.

18
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:15,010
March 13th. Detectives Carroll Cooley
and Wilfred Young bring him in,

19
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:20,050
put him in a lineup, then take him
into Interrogation Room Number 2.

20
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:24,075
About two hours later, they walk
out with a signed confession.

21
00:01:24,817 --> 00:01:27,807
Nobody hit him. Nobody threatened him.

22
00:01:27,887 --> 00:01:32,159
And nobody told him he could
stay silent, or ask for a lawyer.

23
00:01:33,692 --> 00:01:37,082
Here's the detail I love as a lawyer.

24
00:01:37,162 --> 00:01:42,762
He wrote it on a form,
and at the top, already typed,

25
00:01:42,842 --> 00:01:49,334
was a line certifying the statement was
made 'with full knowledge of my legal

26
00:01:49,414 --> 00:01:53,659
rights.' Nobody had told
him what those rights were.

27
00:01:55,042 --> 00:01:58,092
Was that legal in 1963?

28
00:01:58,172 --> 00:02:02,812
In Arizona, yes. The test was
whether a confession was voluntary,

29
00:02:02,892 --> 00:02:05,259
not whether you knew you could refuse.

30
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:09,870
That test came out of
a 1936 case, Brown v.

31
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:14,730
Mississippi. A deputy sheriff and others
whipped three Black men until they

32
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:19,850
confessed to a murder, and the Supreme
Court threw those confessions out.

33
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:24,150
After that, the question was
whether a confession was voluntary,

34
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:29,830
judged case by case: how long,
how old, how educated,

35
00:02:29,910 --> 00:02:32,150
whether anyone made threats.

36
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:36,110
No beating, no threats, two hours.

37
00:02:36,190 --> 00:02:39,492
By that test, Miranda's
confession was fine.

38
00:02:41,025 --> 00:02:47,035
His court-appointed lawyer,
Alvin Moore, objected to the confession.

39
00:02:47,115 --> 00:02:48,595
The judge overruled him.

40
00:02:48,675 --> 00:02:51,695
Convicted. Twenty to thirty years.

41
00:02:51,775 --> 00:02:54,242
The Arizona Supreme Court affirmed.

42
00:02:55,458 --> 00:02:58,488
That should have been the end.

43
00:02:58,568 --> 00:03:03,968
Instead, the ACLU found the case
and brought in two Phoenix lawyers,

44
00:03:04,048 --> 00:03:08,341
John Frank and John Flynn, who took it
for free, all the way to Washington.

45
00:03:08,983 --> 00:03:10,913
They had an opening.

46
00:03:10,993 --> 00:03:14,833
Five days after Miranda signed that
confession, the Supreme Court decided

47
00:03:14,913 --> 00:03:19,933
Gideon v. Wainwright: if you're charged
with a felony and can't afford a lawyer,

48
00:03:20,013 --> 00:03:21,993
the state has to give you one.

49
00:03:22,073 --> 00:03:23,893
A year later came Escobedo v.

50
00:03:23,973 --> 00:03:29,113
Illinois. Police had refused to let a
suspect see the lawyer he was asking for,

51
00:03:29,193 --> 00:03:31,293
and the Court threw out his confession.

52
00:03:31,373 --> 00:03:34,273
That left an obvious question.

53
00:03:34,353 --> 00:03:40,553
If the right to a lawyer matters at trial,
and matters when you ask for one at the

54
00:03:40,633 --> 00:03:45,200
station, what about the person
who doesn't even know to ask?

55
00:03:46,942 --> 00:03:51,912
The Supreme Court bundled Miranda
with three other confession cases.

56
00:03:51,992 --> 00:03:56,784
On June 13, 1966, it ruled five to four.

57
00:04:01,492 --> 00:04:05,362
Chief Justice Earl
Warren's point was simple.

58
00:04:05,442 --> 00:04:08,142
The interrogation room
is built to work on you.

59
00:04:08,222 --> 00:04:11,537
It's private, it's long, and you're alone.

60
00:04:12,192 --> 00:04:15,922
To prove it, he did something unusual.

61
00:04:16,002 --> 00:04:18,682
He quoted the police training manuals.

62
00:04:18,762 --> 00:04:22,262
Question him alone,
on your ground, not his.

63
00:04:22,342 --> 00:04:24,782
Treat his guilt as already settled.

64
00:04:24,862 --> 00:04:28,342
Offer him an excuse that
makes confessing easier.

65
00:04:28,422 --> 00:04:31,522
Play a friendly detective
against a hostile one.

66
00:04:31,602 --> 00:04:36,142
The opinion called it a setting created
for no purpose other than to subjugate the

67
00:04:36,222 --> 00:04:39,117
individual to the will of his examiner.

68
00:04:40,708 --> 00:04:45,018
So before police question somebody in
custody, they have to tell them four

69
00:04:45,098 --> 00:04:46,098
things.

70
00:04:46,650 --> 00:04:48,520
You can stay silent.

71
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:51,040
What you say can be used against you.

72
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:52,760
You can have a lawyer.

73
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,320
And if you can't afford one,
you'll get one.

74
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,908
Skip the warnings, and what he says
can't be used to prove the case.

75
00:05:01,650 --> 00:05:05,740
The four dissenters said it would
put guilty men back on the street.

76
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:11,700
Justice White wrote that 'in some unknown
number of cases the Court's rule will

77
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:16,200
return a killer, a rapist or
other criminal to the streets.'

78
00:05:17,792 --> 00:05:22,467
So did it? Let's follow Ernesto Miranda.

79
00:05:23,108 --> 00:05:28,338
Arizona retried him in 1967,
this time without the confession.

80
00:05:28,418 --> 00:05:31,678
The prosecution called his
former common-law wife.

81
00:05:31,758 --> 00:05:35,898
She testified that during a jail
visit he told her what he'd done.

82
00:05:35,978 --> 00:05:39,450
Convicted again. Twenty to thirty years.

83
00:05:40,667 --> 00:05:44,337
That's the part the TV version leaves out.

84
00:05:44,417 --> 00:05:47,017
Miranda doesn't make evidence disappear.

85
00:05:47,097 --> 00:05:51,817
It takes one thing off the table: what
police get out of you in that room without

86
00:05:51,897 --> 00:05:55,759
the warning. Everything
else is still in play.

87
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:59,030
He was paroled in 1972.

88
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:02,930
Back in Phoenix, he signed the cards
police carry to read the warnings,

89
00:06:03,010 --> 00:06:06,450
and sold them for a dollar or two.

90
00:06:07,817 --> 00:06:12,987
January 31, 1976.
A fight in a Phoenix bar.

91
00:06:13,067 --> 00:06:15,527
Miranda is stabbed and dies.

92
00:06:15,607 --> 00:06:19,227
Police arrest a man they say
handed the killer the knife.

93
00:06:19,307 --> 00:06:21,127
He's read his rights.

94
00:06:21,207 --> 00:06:23,267
Prosecutors declined to charge him.

95
00:06:23,347 --> 00:06:26,827
The man accused of the stabbing
was questioned and let go.

96
00:06:26,907 --> 00:06:29,327
By the time he was charged, he was gone.

97
00:06:29,407 --> 00:06:30,950
He was never tried.

98
00:06:33,817 --> 00:06:37,447
Miranda has been under attack
since the day it came down.

99
00:06:37,527 --> 00:06:43,027
In 1968, Congress passed a law to get
around it: a confession was admissible as

100
00:06:43,107 --> 00:06:44,947
long as it was voluntary.

101
00:06:45,027 --> 00:06:47,727
Then the Court itself started trimming.

102
00:06:47,807 --> 00:06:53,307
In 1971, it said a statement taken without
the warnings can still be used against you

103
00:06:53,387 --> 00:06:56,607
if you take the stand and
tell a different story.

104
00:06:56,687 --> 00:07:03,187
In 1984, it carved out a public safety
exception: if an officer asks where the

105
00:07:03,267 --> 00:07:06,947
gun is before reading the warnings,
the answer comes in.

106
00:07:07,027 --> 00:07:09,742
That opinion was written
by William Rehnquist.

107
00:07:11,275 --> 00:07:17,265
By 2000 he was Chief Justice, and the
1968 law finally reached the Court in

108
00:07:17,345 --> 00:07:21,605
Dickerson. Plenty of people
expected him to finish Miranda off.

109
00:07:21,685 --> 00:07:25,945
Instead he wrote the opinion
saving it, seven to two.

110
00:07:26,025 --> 00:07:31,325
Miranda, he wrote, 'has become embedded in
routine police practice to the point where

111
00:07:31,405 --> 00:07:34,408
the warnings have become part
of our national culture.'

112
00:07:36,417 --> 00:07:38,847
But it isn't what it was.

113
00:07:38,927 --> 00:07:44,227
In 2010, the Court said
staying silent is not enough.

114
00:07:44,307 --> 00:07:46,947
You have to say you're invoking.

115
00:07:47,027 --> 00:07:53,300
And in 2022, it said a Miranda violation
alone doesn't let you sue the officer.

116
00:07:53,942 --> 00:07:56,032
So what does it cost?

117
00:07:56,112 --> 00:07:59,172
Researchers have fought
over the number for decades.

118
00:07:59,252 --> 00:08:05,643
In 1996, Paul Cassell estimated that
Miranda costs prosecutors almost four

119
00:08:05,723 --> 00:08:08,652
percent of all criminal cases.

120
00:08:08,732 --> 00:08:13,252
Stephen Schulhofer went through the
same studies and got about one percent.

121
00:08:13,332 --> 00:08:19,292
What the studies agree on is that
most suspects, about four in five,

122
00:08:19,372 --> 00:08:21,409
waive their rights and talk anyway.

123
00:08:23,150 --> 00:08:26,860
So what does Miranda actually stand for?

124
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:28,075
Three things.

125
00:08:29,025 --> 00:08:32,475
One: the warning isn't required at arrest.

126
00:08:32,555 --> 00:08:35,955
It's required before
custodial interrogation.

127
00:08:36,035 --> 00:08:41,435
Two: the remedy is losing the
statement, not losing the case.

128
00:08:41,515 --> 00:08:45,415
Three: the right is real,
but you have to use it.

129
00:08:45,495 --> 00:08:46,950
Out loud.

130
00:08:48,483 --> 00:08:53,853
If you take one practical thing
from this, the words matter.

131
00:08:53,933 --> 00:09:00,133
In 1994, the Court held that 'Maybe I
should talk to a lawyer' was not clear

132
00:09:00,213 --> 00:09:02,373
enough to stop the questioning.

133
00:09:02,453 --> 00:09:07,013
So be clear. 'I'm going to remain silent.

134
00:09:07,093 --> 00:09:11,393
I want a lawyer.' Then stop talking.

135
00:09:11,473 --> 00:09:15,273
That's general information,
not advice about your case.

136
00:09:15,353 --> 00:09:20,408
If you're ever in that room,
that's exactly when you call one.

137
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,350
Ernesto Miranda was convicted
twice and died in a bar fight.

138
00:09:28,430 --> 00:09:31,930
His name is on the most famous
sentence in American law.

139
00:09:32,010 --> 00:09:36,842
And the right it stands for
only works if you say it.

140
00:09:37,333 --> 00:09:40,883
Next time: the next case.

141
00:09:40,963 --> 00:09:44,633
I'm Eric Hoffman,
and these are The Hoffman Files.

